  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nThe area of steady moderate rain that impacted the area \nthis morning is exiting off to our northeast; however, patchy \ndrizzle continues and will continue on and off through the afternoon \nwhere clouds ceilings are lowest, which is generally from far \neastern South Dakota into southwest Minnesota. Farther west, we've \nseen some breaks in the lowest clouds, allowing for some \ndestabilization to occur. Up to 500-1,000 J/kg of MLCAPE has \ndeveloped per latest SPC Mesoanalysis along the Missouri River \nValley, and as a secondary mid level wave moves across the area, a \nfew weak thunderstorms have already developed over northern Nebraska \nand are lifting northward. Severe weather is not expected given weak \nshear across the area, but we will likely continue to see some \nadditional showers and isolated thunderstorms develop through the \nafternoon and evening. The best chance of rain (30-60%) with this \nactivity will be over western Iowa, far northeast Nebraska, and far \nsoutheast South Dakota, stretching to far southern Minnesota by the \nlate night hours. Tall, skinny CAPE profiles and deep warm cloud \nlayers suggest heavy rain will be the primary threat with any \nthunderstorm. This could lead to a few pockets of ponding in low-\nlying and poor drainage areas, but flash flooding is unlikely. \n\nHeading into tonight, the areas that saw some clearing stratus \n(primarily near and west of the James River) will see some fog \ndevelop, meanwhile the stratus deck elsewhere is expected to lower \nthrough the night and become fog. Locally dense fog is likely given \nall the near surface moisture, though coverage and exact locations \nwhere the densest fog will reside precludes a need for a Dense Fog \nAdvisory at this time, but trends will be monitored. This fog will \ngradually lift through the mid morning hours, but likely become low \nstratus heading into much of the day on Sunday. Still the potential \nfor some of this stratus to mix out a bit and allow for brief breaks \nof sun mainly near and west of the James River, but this will likely \nbe the exception, not the norm across the area. Some instability in \nthe low cloud layer may allow for a few sprinkles to spits of \ndrizzle, but it's not looking as persistent as it has been the past \ncouple of days.\n\nHeading into early next week, a trough over the West Coast will\nbegin to dig into the Desert Southwest. Ahead of this, an upper\nwave will move into the area late Monday into the day on \nTuesday and bring our next round of rain and storms to the area.\nLatest GEFS shows around a 30-40% chance of at least an inch of\nrain mainly focused over northwest Iowa and adjacent areas. This\nis a bit higher than other guidance, but appears to be the most\nreasonable at this time given ensemble PWATs show anomalies \nnearly of +1\" in place as this system moves through. The \naforementioned trough over the Desert Southwest will absorb the \nremnants of a Pacific Ocean tropical cyclone as it ejects to \nthe north and east during the middle to end of the next work. \nThis may help to keep a decent amount of moisture in place over \nprimarily northwestern Iowa into the end of the upcoming work \nweek, though at this point it looks like the heaviest rain\nassociated with this will stay south of the area.",
